I've done a sacrifest loop using a wizard with hungerless
casting and spell of create monster.  All of the numbers
work out the same as with pudding farming.

Building a pile of puddings before a sacrifice that makes
sense for molification.  For prayer timeout it does not.
Sacrifice them one by one as soon as you have one for
prayer timeout purposes.  If you have avoided crowning
sometimes it only takes one sacrifice and the higher the
point value of the monster the better the chance it will only
take one sacrifice.

> I'll catch hell from the same guy who's always jumping on
> me for "wasting time" in game, but I go as far as to collect
> mirrors and hoard them.

I also keep notes on the depth of every co-aligned unicorn.
Mirrors are -2, co-aligned unicorns are -5.  During most of
the game I treat mirrors like valuable gems - I put them in
a box named "Treasure" in case I need them.  Some
games I do a sacrifest loop some games I don't so many
times I just leave the box behind in the late game.  But
the effort price of picking up and stashing mirrors is low
and the AC gain in a sacrifest loop is high.  In fact the AC
gain in a sacrifest loop is so much higher than purchased
protections there have been games I've killed a cross
aligned priest and used the loop rather than paying the
priest.

> See, there's not really an in-game way to know your luck,

While moving luck up this is almost true.  There are plenty
of actions that increase luck by a known amount it's just
that they aren't readily available most of the time.  If you are
at an altar the first sac once your luck is zero moves it up a
random amount so during a sacrifest if you see a clover you
have just lost knowledge of your exact luck.  Time to max
it out again then use fixed number methods to reduce it
back down to the desired number.  It doesn't suck if an
artifact appears while you're doing this ;^) but you'll need to
resume the cycle at the point of pushing your prayer
timout back to zero not maxing out the clovers.

